\par\b\fs30 11. Security audit summary\b0\fs22\par
\par
\par\b\fs26 Existing strengths\b0\fs22\par
\par
- Raw request bytes bounded before multipart parsing.\par
- Per-file/combined upload limits.\par
- Processing slots and wall timeout.\par
- Linux CPU/address-space/core-dump limits for child work.\par
- Temporary upload handles closed on exit paths.\par
- Private worker service.\par
- Private Gotenberg service.\par
- Read-only filesystem/container restrictions.\par
- Dropped capabilities/no-new-privileges.\par
- Resource limits.\par
- Environment proxies disabled on private adapters.\par
- Redirects disabled.\par
- Strict converted-PDF validation.\par
- No document database/cloud history added.\par
- Browser chaining avoids unnecessary server persistence.\par
\par
\par\b\fs26 Remaining security gaps\b0\fs22\par
\par
- Container isolation shares the host kernel.\par
- Public production needs TLS.\par
- Public production needs auth/rate limits/per-user quotas.\par
- Cross-replica/global compute quotas are incomplete.\par
- Retention/deletion policy requires owner decision.\par
- Production monitoring/incident response missing.\par
- Container image scanning/SBOM review remains.\par
- Full hostile-document corpus is incomplete.\par
- Office fidelity/security corpus is small.\par
- Reverse-proxy/edge controls need production design.\par
\par
Treat every PDF and Office file as untrusted.\par
\par
Never log private document contents or passwords.\par
